target:    tests/oracle/classify.py
gate:      composer test:classifier-plants
kind:      restore-defect
expect:    P29-new-errored-outranks-an-oracle-with-no-model label
rationale: Restores the ordering S4 shipped with: the three oracle/arm-availability rules deciding before NEW's own SQL failure, spelled here as the equivalent guard on new-errored so the diff stays one anchor. A NEW arm that fatally errors on a surface the oracle cannot model then reads d-unmodeled/unresolved instead of SQL-error/block - and `unmodeled` is the DEFAULT oracle state through S4-S7, so it is the common case rather than a corner. Failure protocol 6 makes a SQL error on NEW a (b)-candidate that BLOCKS, unconditionally, and says nothing about the oracle. Pinned by three plants (P29/P30/P31) because the old ordering was invisible to all 28 of the original ones: P20 was the only new=error plant and it carried an oracle of class ok, which is the one oracle state under which the two orderings agree.
---
diff --git a/tests/oracle/classify.py b/tests/oracle/classify.py
--- a/tests/oracle/classify.py
+++ b/tests/oracle/classify.py
@@ -296,7 +296,7 @@ RULES = (
         "to know that NEW's query failed, and no register entry may explain a NEW arm that "
         "fails, however the failure is spelled. At position 1 this fires whatever the oracle's "
         "state and whatever OLD did, which is the guarantee the position buys",
-        lambda f: f.new_class == "error",
+        lambda f: f.new_class == "error" and f.oracle_class not in ("unmodeled", "unsupported", "error"),
     ),
     Rule(
         "arm-unsupported", "d-unmodeled",
